// This file contains board patterns that an AI might want to think about
// either making or avoiding on a board. Each board pattern is a rectangular
// grid of cell patterns, much like the Dr. Mario board, except that the grid
// will generally be much smaller than the entire Dr. Mario board and each
// position in the grid will contain a cell pattern rather than a virus, pill
// half, etc.
//
// A cell pattern has:
// * a subset of the colors {blue, red, yellow},
// * a subset of the shapes {virus, left pill half, right pill half,
//   disconnected pill half}, and
// * a subset of special conditions {empty, out of bounds}.
//
// A cell pattern matches a cell on or around a Dr. Mario board if:
// * The cell pattern includes "out of bounds" in its special conditions set
//   and the Dr. Mario board cell is not within the 8x16 playfield, or
// * The cell pattern includes "empty" in its special conditions set and the
//   Dr. Mario board cell is in-bounds but doesn't have anything there, or
// * The Dr. Mario board cell is in-bounds, and has an entity whose color is in
//   the cell pattern's colors set and whose shape is in the cell pattern's
//   shape set. In this last case, a Dr. Mario cell that has an upper pill half
//   or lower pill half is considered to have a disconnected cell half instead.
//
// Board patterns taken as a whole have certain symmetries available: colors
// can be swapped out consistently throughout the board pattern and positions
// can be horizontally mirrored without changing the meaning of the board
// pattern. To explain this in detail, in addition to saying when a board
// pattern matches part of a Dr. Mario board, we'll have a separate concept of
// a direct match between a board pattern and part of a board.
//
// A board pattern directly matches a position on or around a Dr. Mario board
// if:
// * When the board pattern is laid over the board with the board pattern's
//   top-left cell at the given position, at least one of the board pattern's
//   cells is in-bounds, and
// * Every cell pattern in the board pattern matches the Dr. Mario board cell
//   it is laid over.
//
// Given a permutation of colors p, we can convert a board pattern into a new
// board pattern by applying p to each of the color sets in each of the cell
// patterns of the board pattern.
//
// We can also convert a board pattern into a new board pattern by mirroring.
// To mirror a board pattern, first reverse the cell patterns in each row, then
// apply the shape permutation that swaps left pill halves and right pill
// halves to each of the cell patterns' shape sets.
//
// If mirroring is turned off, a board pattern b matches a position on or around
// a Dr. Mario board if there is a color permutation p such that p(b) directly
// matches at that position. If mirroring is turned on, b additionally matches
// a position if mirror(p(b)) directly matches at that position.
//
// A cell pattern can be rendered into a string by listing one character for
// each thing in its three sets (in any order), according to the following table:
//
// b blue
// r red
// y yellow
// x virus
// < left pill half
// > right pill half
// o disconnected pill half
// e empty
// | out of bounds (it's supposed to look like the wall of the bottle)
//
// For example, "byxoe" represents a cell pattern that matches a cell that's
// empty, or that has a virus or disconnected pill half that's blue or yellow.
//
// As a convenience, the cell pattern that matches everything, "bryx<>o|e", can
// also be given as just the string "*".
//
// A board pattern with at least one row can be rendered into a nested list of
// strings by including one list of strings for each row, with the highest row
// coming first. Within a row, include a string for each column. As a
// convenience, trailing "*" cell patterns may be omitted from a row. The
// length of the longest row determines the width of the board pattern.
//
// Annotations about whether a pattern is good or bad are just my educated
// guess. There's nothing forcing a particular AI to agree with me.

// TODO: misc ideas from my napkin (just go ahead and assume they all have an
// implied "and variants/precursors/90 degree rotations/shufflings/reorderings"
// at the end of the description)
// * horizontal-horizontal drop combos:
//     b
//    brr
// * vertical-vertical drop combos:
//     b
//     r
//     r
//     r
//     e
//     b
// * huh. so, if h-h exists and v-v exists, are there h-v and v-h drop combos
//   we should be thinking about?
// * room to finish clears: eebb and ebbe
// * trash on the floor might be worse than trash elsewhere
//      o<>bry
//      o<>br
//      o<>b
//      |
//   variations include shortened versions
//   variations include a little empty space between the trash and the floor --
//   it's probably gonna get to the floor eventually
// * fat logs (don't forget the 90 degree rotations on this one despite the fat
//   log association with being exclusively horizontal)
//     bx b<>o b<>o e
//     rx r<>o r<>o e
//                  bry<>ox|
// * when towering up, only do it on one side
// * is there a way to help it distinguish between "trash" and "trash that's preventing progress on a virus"?
// * this and extensions of it are a sort of fragile build
//      bx
//      b<
//      e

{ "mirroring": true
, "patterns":
[
	// bad: viruses
	[["bx"]]
,
	// good: empty spaces
	[["e"]]
,
	// bad: occupying the cell that makes you lose
	[ ["*","|"]
	, ["|","eb<>o","*","*","br<>o"]
	]
,
	// bad: occupying the cell just below the one that makes you lose
	[ ["*", "|"]
	, ["*", "eb<>o"]
	, ["|","*","*","*","br<>o"]
	]
,
	// bad: occupying the cell two below the one that makes you lose
	[ ["*", "|"]
	, ["*", "eb<>o"]
	, []
	, ["|","*","*","*","br<>o"]
	]
,
	// bad: occupying the cell three below the one that makes you lose
